One common way for vulnerabilities to arise in Java web applications is through **Unsafe Deserialization**. When an application takes untrusted data and attempts to deserialize it using standard Java `ObjectInputStream`, an attacker can provide a specially crafted malicious object to execute arbitrary code on the server.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.URL;

// This method receives data from a web request that is untrusted
public void processRequest(InputStream inputStream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
    // This implementation is vulnerable to deserialization attacks 
    // because it allows an attacker to provide a malicious object stream
    ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);
    Object data = ois.readObject(); 

    System.out.println("Received object: " + data.toString());
}
```

***

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.Arrays;

// A safer approach involves replacing general object deserialization 
// with a data format like JSON or using a Look-Ahead ObjectInputStream.
public void processRequest(InputStream inputStream) throws IOException {
    // Instead of deserializing raw objects, we parse a specific data format (e.g., JSON)
    // Using a library like Jackson or Gson prevents arbitrary object instantiation.
    // This ensures the application only processes the expected data types.

    try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(inputStream))) {
        String line = reader.readLine();
        // Example: Validate that the input strictly follows an expected text pattern
        if (line != null && line.startsWith("{") && line.endsWith("}")) {
            System.out.println("Safely parsed data: " + line);
        } else {
            throw new SecurityException("Invalid data format provided.");
        }
    }
}
```

